Plato: critical Greek editions (@greek-source-plato-critical: 0, is #reference, #part-of @greek-scholarly-sources) { r_28N1ADM4R3DAG5K2898WPKRXCK
Use a critical edition appropriate to the actual dialogue:

- J. Burnet, editor, Platonis Opera, five volumes, Oxford Classical Texts, 1900–1907: the historical Greek edition across the corpus.
- E. A. Duke, W. F. Hicken, W. S. M. Nicoll, D. B. Robinson, and J. C. G. Strachan, editors, Platonis Opera, volume I, Oxford, 1995: Euthyphro, Apology, Crito, Phaedo, Cratylus, Theaetetus, Sophist, and Statesman.
- S. R. Slings, editor, Platonis Rempublicam, Oxford, 2003: the Republic.

[University guide to Plato's critical editions](https://guides.lib.cua.edu/c.php?g=590191&p=4079255) · [Slings' edition in the editor's university record](https://research.vu.nl/en/publications/platonis-rempublicam-recognovit-brevique-adnotatione-critica-inst/).

Record the selected volume, editor, year, and passage. Read the apparatus and editorial conventions rather than silently treating a plain Greek text or a translation as supplying the same information.

**Use:** [the Greek dossier](../research/Philological-study.lingua#r_353E4E4WH3BWFHHJNZMPKBPEXF) and the [Platonic seminars](../research/seminars/Plato-being-and-knowledge.lingua#r_34WRXN1SRY8SZVRKRV7X3X9QWA). **Access:** library, owned edition, or an identified legitimate digitisation; editions have their own reuse terms.
